-/* 0138: 0137 gated sending on usbmuxd's advertised receive window, parsed
- * from th_win on its ACKs under the assumption that it uses the same >>8
- * wire convention as our own outbound window (0123/0135). In practice this
- * stalled every loopback connection outright, syslog included - usbmuxd's
- * synthetic TCP shim does not seem to advertise a real, growing window on
- * this path at all ("usbmuxd does not check sequence numbers" was already
- * the working assumption elsewhere in this file), so the parsed window
- * stayed at (or decoded to) zero forever and nothing was ever allowed to
- * send again. Rather than trust that field, throttle the dumb but reliable
- * way: cap how many chunks a single ru_loop_poll_slot() call forwards, and
- * let the very frequent outer poll loop (ru_tx_poll/ru_rx_poll/ru_loop_poll
- * run every pass of the USB driver's main loop) spread a fast producer's
- * output across many ticks instead of draining the whole socket backlog in
- * one tight inner loop, which is what overran usbmuxd's small
- * per-connection buffer in the first place ("Input buffer overflow ...
- * space=891, payload=1075"). acked_seq/peer_window (0137) are still updated
- * for visibility but no longer gate sending. */
-#define RU_LOOP_CHUNKS_PER_TICK 2u
+/* 0139: 0138's per-tick chunk cap still let up to RU_LOOP_CHUNKS_PER_TICK
+ * full RU_LOOP_RX_CHUNK (1200-byte) reads go out back-to-back with no actual
+ * delay between them - the outer poll loop (ru_tx_poll/ru_rx_poll/
+ * ru_loop_poll, run every pass of the USB driver's main loop) turns out to
+ * cycle fast enough that this was still effectively one big burst, and
+ * usbmuxd's per-connection buffer has been observed as small as ~300 bytes
+ * of headroom ("Input buffer overflow ... space=305, payload=526") - well
+ * under even a single chunk. There is no reliable signal from usbmuxd's side
+ * to size against (0137's parsed window never worked - see its removal in
+ * 0138), so pace by real wall-clock time instead: a simple leaky bucket, one
+ * smaller chunk per call, gated by ru_counter() (already used for ru_delay_us
+ * / ru_sleep_ms) so a connection cannot send again until enough time has
+ * passed for the last chunk to drain at a conservative target rate. This
+ * does not depend on tick frequency, chunk size, or any assumption about
+ * usbmuxd's internals - only wall-clock time, which is the one thing a slow
+ * downstream reader actually needs. */
+#define RU_LOOP_SEND_CHUNK          512u   /* bytes forwarded per allowed send */
+#define RU_LOOP_RATE_BYTES_PER_SEC  16384u /* sustained cap per loopback connection */
 
